# Opportunity OS Improvements Plan

Prepared from a full repository review plus source/API research on 2026-05-28.

This is written as a product + technical execution plan, not a brainstorm. The goal is to turn the current personal MVP into a genuinely useful and sellable opportunity intelligence product for ambitious Indian students and early professionals.

## Research Anchors

Current repo context:

- The app is a Next.js + Supabase product with n8n ingestion, AI extraction, deterministic scoring, email/Telegram digests, application tracking, and admin moderation.
- Existing high-signal source direction is already visible in the n8n audit: Unstop internships converted best; noisy RSS/Reddit/HN/WWR sources had low engagement or low confidence.
- Current weak points are source quality, content usefulness, ranking trust, multi-user operations, and monetizable outcomes.

External source/API notes:

- Greenhouse has a public Job Board API for published jobs and does not require auth for GET endpoints: https://developer.greenhouse.io/job-board.html
- Ashby exposes a public job posting API at `https://api.ashbyhq.com/posting-api/job-board/{JOB_BOARD_NAME}` and supports `includeCompensation=true`: https://developers.ashbyhq.com/docs/public-job-posting-api
- Lever exposes public postings at `https://api.lever.co/v0/postings/{site}?mode=json`: https://github.com/lever/postings-api
- Adzuna provides a jobs API with country search endpoints, including India support through `/jobs/{country}/search/{page}`: https://developer.adzuna.com/activedocs
- Remotive has a public API, but its public API terms require attribution/linkback and explicitly restrict some third-party use cases. Treat this as "use only with clear attribution/permission" rather than a default ingestion source: https://github.com/remotive-io/remote-jobs-api
- Google Programmable Search JSON API exists, but open web search APIs are unstable and not ideal as a primary data backbone. Use site-specific crawling and direct APIs first: https://developers.google.com/custom-search/v1/
- Unstop has public pages/API-backed category pages such as `api.unstop.com/all-opportunities` and `api.unstop.com/hackathons`; the repo already has working Unstop workflows. Treat as a high-value source, but monitor for API changes.

## Executive Diagnosis

The current product is strong for an MVP because it already has the hard skeleton:

- Authentication and onboarding.
- Opportunity storage and personalization.
- Automated ingestion.
- AI parsing.
- Score caching.
- Saved/apply tracker.
- Email and Telegram digest.
- Admin health view.

However, a sellable product is not "a feed of opportunities". Users will only pay if it consistently produces outcomes:

- "I found things I would have missed."
- "The feed is cleaner than Unstop/LinkedIn/WhatsApp groups."
- "I know exactly why this is worth my time."
- "I can apply earlier than everyone else."
- "I get reminders before deadlines."
- "It learns what I care about."

So the product should evolve from an aggregator into an opportunity intelligence system.

The highest leverage improvements are:

1. Build a source-quality engine, not just more scrapers.
2. Add richer opportunity enrichment so every card answers "Should I spend time on this?"
3. Upgrade scoring from generic fit to outcome-aware ranking.
4. Add feedback loops so the system learns from saves, hides, applies, and wins.
5. Make daily digest/alerts feel like a personal analyst, not a dump of links.
6. Move ingestion from fragile local n8n to a production ingestion architecture.
7. Package the product around a clear buyer and willingness-to-pay.

## Product Positioning

### Recommended Positioning

"A personalized opportunity radar for ambitious Indian students and early-career professionals. It finds high-quality internships, competitions, fellowships, hackathons, and early-career roles before deadlines pass, ranks them by fit, and explains what to do next."

### Avoid Positioning It As

- A generic job board.
- A LinkedIn alternative.
- A scraper dashboard.
- "AI recommendations" without proof.

### Sellable Promise

For students:

- Never miss a high-value internship, competition, fellowship, or hackathon.
- See why an opportunity fits you.
- Track deadlines and applications in one place.
- Get alerts only for things worth applying to.

For colleges/campus clubs:

- A curated opportunity intelligence board for students.
- Analytics on what students save/apply to.
- Admin ability to add campus-specific opportunities.

For premium individual users:

- Faster alerts.
- Resume/opportunity match analysis.
- Skill gap and application prep.
- Priority source coverage for target companies/categories.

## North Star Metrics

Do not optimize for total opportunities ingested. That rewards noise.

Primary metric:

- Qualified Actions Per Weekly Active User: count of saves, applies, calendar subscriptions, reminders set, and "not interested" feedback on opportunities with score >= 60.

Activation metric:

- User completes onboarding, saves at least 2 opportunities, and subscribes to one notification channel within 24 hours.

Retention metrics:

- Weekly active users.
- Digest open/click rate.
- Telegram alert click rate.
- Percentage of users who save/apply in week 2 after signup.

Quality metrics:

- Source-to-save rate: saves per 100 opportunities from a source.
- Source-to-apply rate: applies per 100 opportunities from a source.
- Hidden/spam rate.
- Duplicate rate.
- Expired-at-ingestion rate.
- Low-confidence extraction rate.
- User "not interested" rate by source/category.

Business metrics:

- Free-to-premium conversion.
- Premium retention after first month.
- Number of "outcome events": interviews, selected, won, shortlisted.

## Improvement Pillar 1: Source Quality

### Current Problem

The product has workflows for many sources, but the source strategy is still partly opportunistic. Some sources have poor user fit:

- HN Jobs: high-quality jobs, but often senior/US/global, not the target user.
- WeWorkRemotely: large volume but low engagement and remote global noise.
- Reddit: community posts are messy and often not real opportunities.
- Internshala: existing endpoint became stale/deprecated.
- Direct structured sources like Unstop, Devpost, Greenhouse, Lever are stronger because they reduce AI parsing cost and improve freshness.

### Desired Source Portfolio

Split sources into tiers.

Tier 1 - Direct structured, high intent:

- Unstop internships, hackathons, case competitions, scholarships, workshops.
- Devpost hackathons.
- Greenhouse public job boards.
- Ashby public job boards.
- Lever public postings.
- Select official fellowship/scholarship pages.
- College placement/career cell public pages where permitted.

Tier 2 - Semi-structured, curated:

- Company early-career pages.
- YC/Wellfound/startup pages if access is reliable and compliant.
- Public newsletters with RSS.
- Public Telegram/WhatsApp replacement channels only if content can be ingested lawfully and with consent.

Tier 3 - Experimental/noisy:

- Reddit.
- HN jobs.
- Generic remote job boards.
- Search result scraping.

Tier 3 should not feed the main dashboard directly. It should feed a review/learning sandbox until it proves source quality.

